(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "8.2", title: "Networks and the internet", q: "What actually happens when I open a web page?", lessons: [
{ t: "What is a network?", o: "I can explain what a network is and give its benefits and risks.",
  k: ["network: two or more computers connected so they can share data and resources", "resource: something shared, such as a printer, files or an internet connection", "standalone: a computer that is not connected to any other"],
  d: ["List three things you do at school that need computers to be connected.", "For example printing, saving to a shared drive, using the internet, email."],
  s: ["A network is...|Computers connected together|They can exchange data and share resources|The school network connects hundreds of devices",
      "Benefits|Share files and printers|Communicate: email, messages, video calls|Log in from any computer and find your work|Software and backups managed centrally",
      "Risks|Malware can spread from one machine to others|If the network fails, shared services stop|Security is needed to keep data private"],
  a: "Sort the 16 statement cards into benefits and risks of networking, then write advice for a small business deciding whether to network its five computers. Support: sort the first ten cards. Stretch: for each risk, suggest how it could be reduced.",
  w: [["What is a network?", "Two or more computers connected so they can share data and resources."], ["Give three benefits of a network.", "Any three: sharing files, sharing printers, communication, central backup, log in anywhere."], ["Give two risks of a network.", "Any two: malware spreads, failure affects everyone, security threats."], ["What is a standalone computer?", "One that is not connected to any other computer."], ["Explain why a virus is more dangerous on a networked computer than on a standalone one.", "It can copy itself across the network to many other computers."]],
  x: ["Name one resource your school network shares.", "For example a printer, shared drive or internet connection."] },
{ t: "LAN and WAN, wired and wireless", o: "I can compare a LAN with a WAN and wired with wireless connections.",
  k: ["LAN: a local area network covering one site, such as a school", "WAN: a wide area network covering a large geographical area", "wireless: connected by radio signals, without cables"],
  d: ["Is the network in your home the same kind as the network that connects banks across a country? What is different?", "No. The home network covers one building; the bank's covers a huge area."],
  s: ["LAN and WAN|LAN: one site, owned and run by the organisation|WAN: a large area, joining LANs together, using links from telecom companies|The internet is the largest WAN",
      "Wired|Cables carry the data, usually Ethernet|Fast, reliable and harder to intercept|Devices cannot move around",
      "Wireless|Radio signals (Wi-Fi)|Convenient and easy to add devices|Slower at a distance, affected by walls, easier to intercept"],
  a: "Classify ten real networks as LAN or WAN with a reason, then complete the wired and wireless comparison table and recommend one for each of four situations. Support: use the definition cards. Stretch: explain why a school usually uses both wired and wireless.",
  w: [["What does LAN stand for?", "Local area network."], ["What does WAN stand for?", "Wide area network."], ["Give one example of a LAN and one of a WAN.", "LAN: a school or home network. WAN: the internet or a bank's national network."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of wireless.", "Advantage: devices can move, or no cables needed. Disadvantage: slower, less reliable or less secure."], ["A school office computer handles exam data. Recommend wired or wireless and justify it.", "Wired, because it is faster, more reliable and harder to intercept."]],
  x: ["Is Wi-Fi the same thing as the internet?", "No. Wi-Fi is a wireless way of connecting to a network, which may or may not be connected to the internet."] },
{ t: "Lab: map the school network", o: "I can draw a diagram of a network from a description.",
  k: ["network diagram: a drawing that shows devices and how they connect", "server: a computer that provides services to other computers", "client: a computer that requests services from a server"],
  d: ["When you save a file at school, which computer actually stores it?", "A file server, not the computer you are sitting at."],
  s: ["Clients and servers|A server provides a service: files, printing, web pages|A client asks for the service|Your school computer is a client",
      "Reading the clues|Each clue card describes a room and its devices|Work out what connects to what|Count the devices",
      "Drawing|Use a simple symbol for each device and label it|Draw lines for cables, dotted lines for wireless|Add a key"],
  a: "Using the clue cards (or a walk round school with IT support), draw a labelled network diagram showing clients, servers, printers, wireless access points and the internet connection. Support: complete the half-drawn diagram. Stretch: mark the single device whose failure would cause most disruption and explain why.",
  w: [["What is a server?", "A computer that provides services to other computers."], ["What is a client?", "A computer that requests services from a server."], ["Name two services a school server might provide.", "Any two: file storage, printing, email, web filtering, logins."], ["How are wireless links shown on your diagram?", "With dotted lines (as given in the key)."], ["Explain why most schools keep students' files on a server and not on each computer.", "Students can reach their files from any computer, and the files can be backed up and secured in one place."]],
  x: ["How many servers are in your diagram and what does each do?", "A count with roles."] },
{ t: "Network hardware", o: "I can name network hardware and describe what each device does.",
  k: ["router: sends data between networks, such as from a LAN to the internet", "switch: connects devices in a LAN and sends data only to the right one", "NIC: network interface card, the hardware that connects a device to a network"],
  d: ["What is the box at home that your internet connection comes through?", "A router (usually combined with a switch and a wireless access point)."],
  s: ["Inside every device|A network interface card (NIC)|Wired, wireless or both|Each NIC has a unique MAC address",
      "Connecting a LAN|Switch: joins devices and sends data only to the right one|Wireless access point (WAP): lets wireless devices join|Cables link them together",
      "Connecting networks|Router: forwards data between networks|It connects the LAN to the internet|A home 'router' is a router, switch and WAP in one box"],
  a: "Match each device to its job, then label the hardware on the network diagram and explain the route a file takes from a laptop to a printer. Support: use the matching cards. Stretch: explain why a switch is better than simply connecting every computer to every other computer.",
  w: [["What does a router do?", "It sends data between networks, such as from a LAN to the internet."], ["What does a switch do?", "It connects devices in a LAN and sends data only to the intended device."], ["What does NIC stand for, and what is it for?", "Network interface card. It lets a device connect to a network."], ["What does a wireless access point do?", "It lets wireless devices join the network."], ["Explain the difference between a switch and a router.", "A switch connects devices within one network. A router connects different networks together."]],
  x: ["Which three devices are combined in a home 'router' box?", "Router, switch and wireless access point."] },
{ t: "Topologies: star, bus and mesh", o: "I can describe network topologies and compare them.",
  k: ["topology: the way the devices in a network are arranged and connected", "star: every device connects to a central switch", "mesh: devices connect to several others, giving more than one route"],
  d: ["If one road into a town is closed, when can traffic still get in?", "When there is another route."],
  s: ["Star|Every device has its own cable to a central switch|One cable failing affects only one device|If the switch fails, the whole network stops",
      "Bus|All devices share one main cable|Cheap and simple, but a break stops everything|Slows down with many devices. Rare today",
      "Mesh|Devices connect to several others|Data can take another route if a link fails|More cables or radios, so more cost. The internet works this way"],
  a: "Draw each topology for six devices and count the connections. Complete the comparison table for cost, reliability and what happens when one part fails. Support: label the three ready-drawn diagrams. Stretch: work out how many links a full mesh of 6 and of 10 devices needs.",
  w: [["What is a topology?", "The way the devices in a network are arranged and connected."], ["In a star network, what is at the centre?", "A switch."], ["What happens in a star network if one cable breaks?", "Only the device on that cable loses its connection."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of a mesh network.", "Advantage: alternative routes, so it is reliable. Disadvantage: more connections, so more expensive."], ["Explain why most school networks use a star topology.", "A single cable fault affects one device only, faults are easy to find, and devices can be added without disturbing the rest."]],
  x: ["Which topology gives more than one route between devices?", "Mesh."] },
{ t: "Lab: build a human network", o: "I can model how data travels through different network layouts.",
  k: ["model: a simplified version of something real", "route: the path data takes", "failure: when a device or link stops working"],
  d: ["In a star network of students holding string to one person in the middle, what happens if the middle person lets go?", "Nobody can pass messages: the network fails."],
  s: ["Build it|Students are devices; string is cable|Build a star, then a bus, then a mesh|Pass message cards along the string only",
      "Test it|Time a message from one side to the other|Cut one link: can the message still arrive?|Remove the centre of the star",
      "Record|Number of links|What failed when a link was cut|Which layout coped best"],
  a: "In groups of eight, build each topology with string, pass three messages and record the results. Then break one link in each and try again. Support: follow the role cards. Stretch: design a layout that keeps working when any two links are cut, using as little string as possible.",
  w: [["In the human star network, who represents the switch?", "The person in the middle."], ["What happened in the bus network when the string was cut?", "Messages could not pass the break."], ["Which network still worked after a link was cut?", "The mesh."], ["Which network used the most string?", "The mesh."], ["Explain one way the string model is not like a real network.", "For example real data travels almost instantly, messages are split into packets, or devices do not get tired or drop the cable."]],
  x: ["Which topology would you choose for a hospital, and why?", "A mesh or a star with backup links, because reliability matters most."] },
{ t: "The internet and the World Wide Web", o: "I can explain the difference between the internet and the World Wide Web.",
  k: ["internet: the worldwide network of connected networks", "World Wide Web: the collection of web pages reached over the internet", "web browser: software that requests and displays web pages"],
  d: ["Are 'the internet' and 'the web' the same thing? What do you think?", "No. The web is one of the services that uses the internet."],
  s: ["The internet|A network of networks, joined across the world|It is the hardware: cables, routers, links under the sea|Nobody owns all of it",
      "The World Wide Web|Web pages and sites, linked by hyperlinks|Stored on web servers|Viewed with a web browser",
      "One of many services|The web uses the internet|So do email, video calls, online games and file transfer|The internet is the roads; the web is one kind of traffic"],
  a: "Sort 14 items (router, website, email, undersea cable, hyperlink, browser and others) into 'part of the internet', 'part of the web' or 'another service using the internet'. Then write an explanation for a younger pupil using the road analogy. Support: sort with the definitions card. Stretch: research one service that uses the internet but is not the web, and describe how it differs.",
  w: [["What is the internet?", "The worldwide network of connected networks."], ["What is the World Wide Web?", "The collection of web pages reached over the internet."], ["Name two services, other than the web, that use the internet.", "Any two: email, video calls, online games, file transfer, streaming."], ["What does a web browser do?", "It requests web pages and displays them."], ["Explain the statement: 'You can use the internet without using the web.'", "Sending an email or playing an online game uses the internet's connections but does not involve web pages."]],
  x: ["Complete: the internet is the ____; the web is ____ that travels on it.", "roads (hardware); one kind of traffic (service)."] },
{ t: "IP addresses and MAC addresses", o: "I can describe what IP and MAC addresses are for.",
  k: ["IP address: a number that identifies a device on a network so data can be sent to it", "MAC address: a unique number built into a network interface card", "unique: the only one of its kind"],
  d: ["What two pieces of information does a letter need on the envelope?", "The address it is going to and the address it came from."],
  s: ["IP addresses|Every device on a network needs one|Written as four numbers from 0 to 255: 192.168.1.20|It can change when you join a different network",
      "MAC addresses|Built into the network card when it is made|Written in hexadecimal: 3C:22:FB:9A:10:4E|It normally never changes",
      "Why both?|The IP address is like a postal address: it says where you are now|The MAC address is like a name: it says which device you are|Routers use IP addresses; switches use MAC addresses"],
  a: "Decide which of the ten addresses are valid IP addresses and explain what is wrong with the others. Find your computer's IP address (with your teacher). Complete the IP and MAC comparison table. Support: check each address against the two rules on the card. Stretch: an IP address is four numbers each stored in 8 bits. How many addresses are possible?",
  w: [["What is an IP address for?", "To identify a device on a network so that data can be sent to it."], ["What is a MAC address?", "A unique number built into a network interface card."], ["Which of these is a valid IP address: 192.168.1.300 or 10.0.0.25? Why?", "10.0.0.25. Each number must be from 0 to 255, and 300 is too large."], ["Which address can change when you take a laptop to a different network?", "The IP address."], ["Explain why a device needs both an IP address and a MAC address.", "The IP address locates the device on the network it is currently in. The MAC address identifies the actual hardware, so the switch can deliver data to it."]],
  x: ["Which is written in hexadecimal: an IP address or a MAC address?", "A MAC address."] },
{ t: "Lab: trace a route", o: "I can show that data crosses many routers to reach a website.",
  k: ["hop: one step from a router to the next", "route: the series of hops data takes", "latency: the delay before data arrives"],
  d: ["A website's server is on another continent. How do you think your request gets there?", "It is passed from router to router along cables, including cables under the sea."],
  s: ["Tracing a route|A trace tool lists every router on the way to a site|Each line is one hop|It also shows the time taken",
      "Reading the results|The first hops are inside your school or internet provider|Later hops may be in other countries|More hops usually means more delay",
      "Plotting|Record the number of hops for five sites|Mark the likely countries on a map|Compare a local site with a distant one"],
  a: "With your teacher, run (or study the printed results of) a route trace to five websites. Record the hops and times, plot the routes on the world map and write three observations. Support: use the printed results for two sites. Stretch: run the same trace twice and explain why the route can differ.",
  w: [["What is a hop?", "One step from a router to the next."], ["What is latency?", "The delay before data arrives."], ["Which site had the most hops in your results?", "A named site with a number."], ["Why does a distant website usually respond more slowly?", "The data crosses more routers and travels further."], ["Explain how the trace shows that the internet is a network of networks.", "The route passes through routers belonging to different organisations and countries on the way."]],
  x: ["Roughly how many hops did it take to reach the furthest site?", "A number."] },
{ t: "URLs and DNS", o: "I can explain the parts of a URL and what DNS does.",
  k: ["URL: the address of a web page", "domain name: the name of a website, such as example.com", "DNS: the system that finds the IP address for a domain name"],
  d: ["Do you know your best friend's phone number by heart, or do you look up their name?", "Most people look up the name. DNS does the same job for websites."],
  s: ["Parts of a URL|`https://www.example.com/news/today.html`|Protocol: https|Domain name: www.example.com|Path: /news/today.html",
      "Why DNS is needed|Computers find each other by IP address|People remember names, not numbers|DNS translates the name into the number",
      "How a lookup works|The browser asks a DNS server for the IP address of the domain|If that server does not know, it asks another|The IP address is sent back and remembered for next time"],
  a: "Label the parts of eight URLs. Then put the six DNS lookup cards in order and explain what would happen if DNS stopped working. Support: label the first four URLs using the colour key. Stretch: explain why a site can move to a new server without its address changing for users.",
  w: [["What does URL stand for, and what is it?", "Uniform resource locator: the address of a web page."], ["In `https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport`, what is the domain name?", "www.bbc.co.uk"], ["What does DNS do?", "It finds the IP address for a domain name."], ["Why do we use domain names instead of IP addresses?", "Names are easier for people to remember than numbers."], ["Explain what a user would notice if the DNS servers failed, and why.", "Typing a site name would not load the page, because the name could not be turned into an IP address. Typing the IP address directly could still work."]],
  x: ["DNS is often called the internet's ____.", "phone book (address book)."] },
{ t: "What happens when you load a page", o: "I can describe, step by step, how a web page reaches my browser.",
  k: ["request: a message asking a server for something", "response: the message a server sends back", "web server: a computer that stores web pages and sends them when requested"],
  d: ["Put in order: the page is displayed; you type the address; the server sends the page.", "Type the address; the server sends the page; the page is displayed."],
  s: ["The steps|1 You type a URL or click a link|2 The browser asks DNS for the IP address|3 The browser sends a request to the web server at that address",
      "The reply|4 The web server finds the page|5 It sends the page back as a response|6 The browser renders the HTML into the page you see",
      "More than one request|The HTML lists images, styles and scripts|The browser requests each of them too|One page can need dozens of requests"],
  a: "Order the eight step cards, then draw and annotate a diagram of the whole journey showing the browser, DNS server, routers and web server. Support: arrange the cards on the outline diagram. Stretch: add what happens when the page contains three images.",
  w: [["What is a web server?", "A computer that stores web pages and sends them when requested."], ["What does the browser need from DNS before it can request a page?", "The IP address of the web server."], ["What is the message from the browser to the server called?", "A request."], ["What does the browser do with the HTML it receives?", "It renders it: turns the code into the page you see."], ["Describe the journey of a web page from typing the URL to seeing the page.", "The browser asks DNS for the server's IP address, sends a request to the web server, the server sends the page back, and the browser renders it."]],
  x: ["How many steps can you list from memory?", "Six steps in the correct order."] },
{ t: "Lab: role-play a page request", o: "I can act out and explain every stage of a web request.",
  k: ["role-play: acting out a process to understand it", "sequence: the order in which things happen", "bottleneck: the slowest part, which holds everything else up"],
  d: ["Which three 'characters' are involved in loading a web page?", "The browser, the DNS server and the web server."],
  s: ["Roles|Browser, DNS server, routers, web server|Each has a script card|Messages are written on paper and passed by hand",
      "Run it|Request a page by name|DNS gives the address|Routers pass the request on, and the response back",
      "Break it|Remove the DNS server|Give a wrong address|Make one router very slow: what happens?"],
  a: "In groups of six, act out three page requests using the role cards and message slips, then run the three 'break it' experiments and record what the user would see. Support: play one role with a partner. Stretch: add a second browser requesting a different page at the same time.",
  w: [["Which role turned the site name into an address?", "The DNS server."], ["Which role stored the page?", "The web server."], ["What happened when the DNS server was removed?", "The browser could not find the server's address, so the page did not load."], ["What happened when one router was slow?", "Everything passing through it was delayed: a bottleneck."], ["Explain what the user sees when the address is wrong, and why.", "An error such as 'page not found' or 'site cannot be reached', because no server at that address holds the page."]],
  x: ["Which stage of the request did you understand better after acting it out?", "A named stage."] },
{ t: "Packets and packet switching", o: "I can describe how data is sent across a network in packets.",
  k: ["packet: a small block of data sent across a network", "packet switching: sending packets separately, each by its own route", "header: the part of a packet that holds the addresses and packet number"],
  d: ["You need to post a 1000-piece jigsaw but only have small envelopes. What do you do, and what do you write on each?", "Split it between envelopes, each with the address and a number so it can be put back together."],
  s: ["Packets|Files are split into small packets|Each packet has a header and a payload|Header: sender's address, receiver's address, packet number",
      "Packet switching|Each packet is sent separately|Routers choose the best route for each one|Packets may take different routes and arrive out of order",
      "At the other end|The receiver uses the packet numbers to put them in order|A missing packet is requested again|The file is rebuilt exactly"],
  a: "Split the message into numbered packets with headers, 'send' them by different routes across the classroom and reassemble them. Then answer the questions on what the header is for. Support: use the packet templates. Stretch: one packet is lost. Write what the receiver and sender each do.",
  w: [["What is a packet?", "A small block of data sent across a network."], ["Name three things in a packet header.", "The sender's address, the receiver's address and the packet number."], ["Why might packets arrive out of order?", "They can take different routes of different lengths."], ["How does the receiver put the packets back in order?", "It uses the packet numbers in the headers."], ["Explain one advantage of sending data in packets instead of as one large block.", "If a route is busy or broken, packets can go another way, and only a lost packet needs resending, not the whole file."]],
  x: ["What is the part of a packet that carries the actual data called?", "The payload."] },
{ t: "Protocols: agreeing the rules", o: "I can explain what a protocol is and compare HTTP with HTTPS.",
  k: ["protocol: an agreed set of rules for communication", "HTTP: the protocol used to request and send web pages", "HTTPS: HTTP with the data encrypted"],
  d: ["When you answer a phone call, what do both people say first? Why?", "'Hello' and who they are. It is an agreed way to start, so both know the call has begun."],
  s: ["Protocols|Rules both sides agree to follow|They cover how to start, what to send and how to finish|Without them, devices from different makers could not communicate",
      "HTTP|HyperText Transfer Protocol|The browser sends a request; the server sends a response|Data is sent as plain text that could be read on the way",
      "HTTPS|The same, but encrypted|Anyone intercepting it sees scrambled data|Look for https and the padlock before typing passwords or card details"],
  a: "Match the protocols to their jobs, then compare what an eavesdropper sees on HTTP and on HTTPS using the two example printouts. Decide which of eight activities need HTTPS. Support: match using the clue cards. Stretch: explain why the padlock shows the connection is private but not that the website is honest.",
  w: [["What is a protocol?", "An agreed set of rules for communication."], ["What is HTTP used for?", "Requesting and sending web pages."], ["What is the difference between HTTP and HTTPS?", "HTTPS encrypts the data so it cannot be read if intercepted."], ["Name two activities for which HTTPS is essential.", "Any two: logging in, online banking, shopping, sending personal details."], ["Explain why two computers from different manufacturers can communicate over the internet.", "They follow the same protocols, so each understands the messages of the other."]],
  x: ["What should you check in the address bar before typing a password?", "That the address begins with https (padlock shown)."] },
{ t: "Lab: packet-switching simulator", o: "I can investigate packet switching and explain what I observe.",
  k: ["simulator: a program that imitates a real system", "sequence number: the number that gives a packet's place in the message", "retransmission: sending a packet again because it did not arrive"],
  d: ["Why does each packet carry a sequence number?", "So the receiver can put the packets in the right order and spot any that are missing."],
  s: ["The simulator|Open Networks, then Packet switching, in CS Toolkit|Type a message and split it into packets|Step through one hop at a time",
      "Observe|Which route does each packet take?|In what order do they arrive?|Compare arrival order with sequence numbers",
      "Lose a packet|Tick 'Lose one packet on the way'|Watch what the receiver does|How does it know which one is missing?"],
  a: "Run the simulator five times with the same message and record each packet's route and arrival order. Then run it with a lost packet and describe what happens. Write three conclusions. Support: record two runs using the results table. Stretch: explain why the same message takes different routes on different runs, and why that makes the internet reliable.",
  w: [["Did all packets take the same route?", "No. Different packets took different routes."], ["Did the packets always arrive in order?", "No."], ["How did the receiver rebuild the message?", "By sorting the packets by sequence number."], ["What did the receiver do when a packet was lost?", "It noticed the gap in the sequence numbers and asked for that packet again."], ["Explain why packet switching still works when one router fails.", "Routers send packets by another route, so the data still arrives."]],
  x: ["Write one conclusion from your investigation.", "A conclusion supported by the results."] },
{ t: "Revision: annotate a page request", o: "I can bring together everything in the unit in one diagram.",
  k: ["annotate: to add notes that explain a diagram", "revise: to go over learning again", "link: to connect ideas together"],
  d: ["List five key words from this unit and say one in a sentence.", "For example router, DNS, packet, protocol, IP address."],
  s: ["Hardware|NIC, switch, wireless access point, router|LAN inside the building, WAN beyond|Star topology in school, mesh across the internet",
      "Finding the server|URL: protocol, domain name, path|DNS turns the domain name into an IP address|IP addresses locate devices; MAC addresses identify hardware",
      "Sending the data|Request and response follow HTTP or HTTPS|Data is split into packets with headers|Packets are routed separately and reassembled"],
  a: "On the large page-request diagram, annotate every stage from typing the URL to the page appearing, using at least 12 key words. Then answer the six-mark practice question and mark it with the levels rubric. Support: place the 12 key-word labels on the diagram. Stretch: add what changes when the page is requested over HTTPS.",
  w: [["Name the device that connects a LAN to the internet.", "A router."], ["What does DNS return?", "The IP address for a domain name."], ["What three things are in a packet header?", "Sender's address, receiver's address, packet number."], ["Which protocol encrypts web traffic?", "HTTPS."], ["Explain how a web page reaches your browser. Use the words DNS, IP address, request and packets.", "The browser asks DNS for the server's IP address, sends a request to that address, and the server sends the page back split into packets, which are reassembled and displayed."]],
  x: ["Which key word are you least confident about?", "A named key word."] },
{ t: "Assessed test and extended answer", o: "I can show what I know about networks and write an extended answer.",
  k: ["extended answer: a longer answer that needs linked points in a logical order", "describe: to say what something is or does", "explain: to say how or why"],
  d: ["What makes a six-mark answer different from a one-mark answer?", "It needs several correct points, linked together in a sensible order, using key words."],
  s: ["The test|Short questions on hardware, addresses and protocols|One diagram to label|One six-mark extended answer",
      "Writing the extended answer|Plan four to six points in order|Use the key words accurately|Write in full sentences",
      "Check|Have you answered the question asked?|Is every key word used correctly?|Is the order logical?"],
  a: "Complete the written test under test conditions, including the six-mark question on how a web page reaches a browser. Support: a key-word bank is provided for the extended answer. Stretch: attempt the extension question on why the internet uses a mesh.",
  w: [["State the difference between a LAN and a WAN.", "A LAN covers one site. A WAN covers a large geographical area."], ["Give the job of a switch.", "It connects devices in a LAN and sends data only to the intended device."], ["State what DNS does.", "It finds the IP address for a domain name."], ["Describe what happens to a file before it is sent across the internet.", "It is split into packets, each with a header holding addresses and a packet number."], ["Explain why HTTPS should be used for online banking.", "The data is encrypted, so login details and account information cannot be read if they are intercepted."]],
  x: ["How many separate points did you make in your extended answer?", "A number from 4 to 6."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can improve an extended answer and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["feedback: comments that help you improve", "redraft: to write an improved version", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["Read your extended answer. Underline every key word you used.", "A self-check of key words."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Find your level for the extended answer|Which points were missing?|Was the order logical?",
      "Redraft|Add the missing points|Replace vague words with key words|Check each sentence follows from the last",
      "Reflect|Which idea surprised you most?|What can I explain now that I could not before?|What is my target?"],
  a: "Redraft your extended answer using the feedback and the model answer, then complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has listed the two points to add. Stretch: write a six-mark question of your own with a mark scheme.",
  w: [["Which points were missing from your extended answer?", "A list of points."], ["Write your redrafted answer.", "An improved answer with linked points and key words."], ["Name one thing you can explain now that you could not six weeks ago.", "A unit idea."], ["Which idea in the unit surprised you most?", "A reasoned choice."], ["Set one target for extended answers in future tests.", "A specific target."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, what actually happens when you open a web page?", "The browser uses DNS to find the server's IP address, sends a request, and receives the page in packets that are reassembled and displayed."] }
